system "ISO:hye-Armn:Latn:9985-2026" {

metadata {
  authority_id iso
  id 9985-2026
  language iso-639-2:hye
  source_script Armn
  destination_script Latn
  name "ISO 9985:2026 Information and documentation -- Transliteration of Armenian characters into Latin characters"
  url https://www.iso.org/committee/48820.html
  # Source document: interscript-private-references/iso/ISO-9985-2026.pdf
  # (purchased published edition, ISO Store order OP-1130631, downloaded
  # 2026-11-19; verification-grade provenance).
  creation_date 2026
  adoption_date 2026
  description {    Second edition (cancels and replaces ISO 9985:1996; no map for the
    first edition exists in the corpus). Table 1 transliterates all 38
    letters of the Armenian alphabet, each upper/lower case pair to its
    upper/lower Latin pair, with the aspirate rows (Թ Չ Ց Փ Ք) carrying
    the neutral (vertical line) apostrophe U+0027 - the edition's stated
    change is that all aspirates now share the one visual convention
    (formerly a right single quotation mark U+2019) and the unaspirated
    pairs Ծ/Ճ are now shown unmarked like their p/t/k counterparts.
    The yew ligature և is transliterated as the digraph /ew/; the ու
    letter sequence romanizes /ow/ character by character. Table 2
    transliterates the eight Armenian punctuation marks.
  }

  notes {
    note "Encoded from the purchased published edition (ISO Store order OP-1130631, downloaded 2026-11-19); the PDF text layer is clean and the hex column is authoritative over the printed glyphs. Extracted 2026-11-19."
    note "Case-mapping quirk preserved exactly as the standard's hex column prints it: uppercase J-caron (the Ջ row) is the decomposed pair U+004A+U+030C because no precomposed uppercase form exists, while the lowercase row is the precomposed U+01F0; 4.1 requires systems to treat precomposed and decomposed input equally."
    note "Reverse-direction ambiguity, flagged per the source: forward, both the ligature և (Table 1 provision) and the two-letter sequence ե+ւ produce /ew/, while the document states the system is entirely reversible. This map reverses /ew/ to the ligature և (the standard now treats it as a distinct digraph character of the alphabet); a source sequence ե+ւ therefore does not round-trip. The ու sequence has no ligature row and round-trips as ow."
    note "Table 2 punctuation, hex column authoritative: row 1 is the identity comma U+002C; row 2 maps the Armenian middle mark (U+2024, the one dot leader as printed in the hex column) to the semicolon; the Armenian full stop U+0589 maps to the full stop; the question/exclamation marks and the shesht (U+055B, to modifier circumflex U+02C6) and hyphen (U+058A) follow the printed column."
    note "Edition difference (1996 -> 2026), from the 2026 Foreword: aspirates moved from the right single quotation mark U+2019 to the neutral U+0027, and the unaspirated convention was extended to the Ծ (c) and Ճ (c-caron) pairs, formerly shown as c'/c-caron-apostrophe. The 1996 document is not held; no legacy map exists to verify against (Armenia in the corpus is covered by din-hye-Armn-Latn-32706-2010, a different, untouched system)."
    note "No capitalization provisions: Armenian distinguishes upper and lower case and Table 1 maps each case explicitly; document examples print capitalized name forms only as optional target-language convention."
  }
}

tests {
   "և" ->  "ew"
   "ու" ->  "ow"
   "Երևան" ->  "Erewan"
   "Հայաստան" ->  "Hayastan"
   "ՀԱՅԱՍՏԱՆ" ->  "HAYASTAN"
